# sharkd's global preferences, read from the data directory of the Wireshark
# installed next to it - so every program in this image starts with these columns.
#
# The only reason the file exists: the columns. sharkd will not add one on request
# - `frames` takes no column list, and `setconf gui.column.format` after startup
# renames the columns without changing what they hold - so every column the UI can
# ever want has to be a column from the very start. Four things want one:
#
# - Source and Destination carry addresses but not ports, and both the packet list
#   and the sequence-diagram view want them, as Wireshark's own flow graph labels
#   each arrow end with its port. SrcPort/DstPort sit right after Source/Destination
#   so the packet list shows address and port together; the flow view reads the same
#   columns out of the rows it already has.
#
# - The outer end of a tunnelled frame. Source and Destination hold the
#   *innermost* address the frame has, which inside GTP-U is the UE's own - a leg
#   this capture never carried as a packet of its own, while the hop it did carry
#   is the outer one. So four more hidden columns hold the outer address and port
#   of each end, and app.js draws those instead wherever they differ; see cell()
#   there. A custom column's occurrence 1 is the first of that field in the frame,
#   and the first is the outermost - which is the whole trick.
#
#   The field list is `ip.src or ipv6.src` because either family can carry the
#   tunnel, and occurrence 1 is taken once per field - so a tunnel that changes
#   family fills the column with both, inner and outer in the order the list names
#   them. Which is still enough: the innermost is what the plain column already
#   holds, so the other of the two is the outer one whichever way round it came.
#   A frame with no IP at all leaves these blank and keeps the plain column - a
#   MAC, a resolved name - as it was.
#
# - The settings drawer offers the Time column in three shapes, and a shape is a
#   column format - so all three are here, and the drawer picks which one the list
#   draws in the Time slot. Delta and UTC are marked hidden below: sharkd still
#   sends their values with every row, and reports them as `visible: false`, which
#   is what keeps them out of the list until they are asked for.
#
# - Expert info, which is Wireshark's own judgement on a frame: a checksum that
#   does not add up, a retransmission, an AVP no dictionary has. It is in the
#   dissection tree already - and the tree is one frame at a time, while the list
#   is where the frame worth opening has to be spotted. So the severity of every
#   expert item on a frame and the summary of each are two more hidden columns,
#   and the row wears the worst of them down its left edge. See expert() in
#   app.js. Occurrence 0 is every item on the frame rather than the first, which
#   is what makes the worst of them findable at all.
#
# Everything else below is Wireshark's default column set, unchanged.

# The two alternative Time columns, the two expert ones and the four outer ones,
# by format string - see the note above. A custom column is written here as it is
# written in the format list below, display suffix and all; sharkd reports it back
# without that suffix - `%Cus:_ws.expert.severity:0` - which is the spelling app.js
# looks for.
gui.column.hidden: %Gt,%Yut,%Cus:_ws.expert.severity:0:R,%Cus:_ws.expert.message:0:R,%Cus:ip.src or ipv6.src:1,%Cus:udp.srcport or tcp.srcport or sctp.srcport:1,%Cus:ip.dst or ipv6.dst:1,%Cus:udp.dstport or tcp.dstport or sctp.dstport:1

gui.column.format:
	"No.", "%m",
	"Time", "%t",
	"Delta", "%Gt",
	"UTC", "%Yut",
	"Source", "%s",
	"SrcPort", "%uS",
	"Destination", "%d",
	"DstPort", "%uD",
	"Protocol", "%p",
	"Length", "%L",
	"Info", "%i",
	"Expert", "%Cus:_ws.expert.severity:0:R",
	"Expert info", "%Cus:_ws.expert.message:0:R",
	"OuterSrc", "%Cus:ip.src or ipv6.src:1",
	"OuterSrcPort", "%Cus:udp.srcport or tcp.srcport or sctp.srcport:1",
	"OuterDst", "%Cus:ip.dst or ipv6.dst:1",
	"OuterDstPort", "%Cus:udp.dstport or tcp.dstport or sctp.dstport:1"

# ESP, because Gm is protected with it: 3GPP runs IPsec ESP in transport mode
# between the UE and the P-CSCF (TS 33.203), so every SIP message after the
# registration is behind an ESP header and Wireshark leaves all three of these
# off by default. Turned on, a protected Gm frame reads as SIP - packet list,
# dissection tree, flow diagram and ims.lua alike.
#
# The two decode preferences are one mechanism and the heuristic is another; both
# are wanted, because they cover different frames:
#
# - The heuristic needs no keys at all. It only applies when the encryption
#   algorithm really is NULL - which is what this stack negotiates, `ealg=null`
#   in the Security-Server header - and finds the payload by checking that what
#   trails it looks like ESP padding. Left at `esp.padding: rfc`, so a frame that
#   pads any other way is not guessed at.
# - enable_encryption_decode is the keyed path, over the SAs in the esp_sa table:
#   it decrypts (or, for a NULL SA, just trims the trailer) and dissects what
#   comes out. plugins/ims_esp fills that table from the capture, so it covers
#   encrypted Gm as well - see the comment at the top of that plugin.
#
# enable_authentication_check is the check on all of that: with an SA matched it
# verifies the ICV with the SA's integrity key and says so in the tree, which is
# how a key that came out of the capture wrong is visible instead of silent.
#
# A frame with an SA is dissected by the keyed path and not by the heuristic, so
# the two decode preferences go together: with the check on and the decode off, a
# frame whose SA matches stops at "Data" - the heuristic does not pick it up
# again. Frames with no SA at all are unaffected and stay with the heuristic.
esp.enable_null_encryption_decode_heuristic: TRUE
esp.enable_encryption_decode: TRUE
esp.enable_authentication_check: TRUE
esp.do_esp_sequence_analysis: FALSE
